# factory.env: settings for your own software factory.
#
#   cp factory.env.example factory.env    # factory.env is gitignored
#
# Only SF_ACCOUNT and SF_PROJECT are required. Everything commented out shows
# its default (from scripts/config.sh); uncomment to change it.

# ============================================================================
# Google Cloud
# ============================================================================

# The gcloud account that owns the VM. Must be logged in locally:
#   gcloud auth login you@example.com
SF_ACCOUNT=""

# GCP project ID the VM is created in (not the display name). List yours with:
#   gcloud projects list
# The project needs the Compute Engine API enabled and billing attached.
SF_PROJECT=""

# Zone the VM runs in. Pick one near you: latency matters for a terminal you
# type into all day. Pricing varies a little by region.
#   gcloud compute zones list
# SF_ZONE="northamerica-northeast2-a"        # Toronto

# ============================================================================
# The VM
# ============================================================================

# VM name. Also its hostname, its name on your tailnet, the SSH host alias
# (`ssh <name>`), and the prefix for its firewall rules. Defaults to
# include your local username so teammates sharing a project don't collide.
# SF_NAME="software-factory-$USER"

# Machine size. e2-standard-4 is 4 vCPU / 16 GB, about $0.15/hour running.
# Agents running builds and tests in parallel may want e2-standard-8
# (8 vCPU / 32 GB, about $0.30/hour). You pay only while it is running.
#   https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/general-purpose-machines#e2_machine_types
# SF_MACHINE_TYPE="e2-standard-4"

# Boot disk size in GB. Billed even while stopped (about $0.10/GB/month), so
# 50 GB is about $5/month. Can be grown later, not shrunk.
# SF_DISK_GB="50"

# OS image. The provision/ scripts assume Ubuntu (apt); change with care.
# SF_IMAGE_FAMILY="ubuntu-2404-lts-amd64"
# SF_IMAGE_PROJECT="ubuntu-os-cloud"

# ============================================================================
# Login
# ============================================================================

# Your username on the VM.
# SF_USER="$USER"

# The SSH public key allowed to log in. It is the only one: project-wide keys
# in GCP are blocked on this VM. Use a key with a passphrase, loaded in your
# ssh-agent (ssh-add). To make one: ssh-keygen -t ed25519
# SF_PUBKEY="$HOME/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub"

# ============================================================================
# Remote access
# ============================================================================

# How you reach the VM:
#
#   ssh        Port 22 is open, but only to your current public IP. When your
#              IP changes (new network, VPN), run ./factory allow-ip. The VM's
#              public IP also changes on every start; ./factory start rewrites
#              your SSH config for you.
#
#   tailscale  Port 22 is closed to the internet entirely; you reach the VM
#              over your tailnet by name. Needs Tailscale on your machine.
#              A brand-new VM still bootstraps over ssh (Tailscale isn't on it
#              yet); ./factory provision closes port 22 once it has joined.
#              If Tailscale ever breaks, ./factory allow-ip reopens port 22 to
#              your IP as a way back in.
#
# Start with ssh if you don't use Tailscale yet. To switch later, change this
# and run ./factory access.
SF_ACCESS="ssh"

# Tailscale auth key, so a new VM joins your tailnet without a browser login.
# Optional: without it, provisioning prints a login URL and waits for you.
# Create one at https://login.tailscale.com/admin/settings/keys (a reusable,
# pre-approved key is easiest). Sent to the VM over stdin, never on a command
# line, and not stored on the VM.
# TS_AUTHKEY=""

# ============================================================================
# Apps on the VM
# ============================================================================

# Ovis (provision/70-ovis.sh) sends anonymous usage counts by default. Set to
# 0 to turn them off, then re-run: ./factory provision 70-ovis.sh
#   https://github.com/devswha/ovis/blob/main/docs/guide.md#anonymous-usage-counts
# SF_OVIS_TELEMETRY="1"

# ============================================================================
# GitHub issues -> draft PRs (provision/55-github.sh, /factory-issue in pi)
# ============================================================================

# Repos the agent works on, as space-separated owner/repo. Each is cloned to
# ~/work/<owner>/<repo> on the VM.
# SF_FACTORY_REPOS=""                       # e.g. "dembrane/echo"

# The label you put on an issue to queue it for the agent. `./factory issues`
# lists open issues with it, and /factory-issue refuses issues without it.
# Defaults to your username, so teammates on one repo each have their own.
# SF_FACTORY_LABEL="factory:$USER"

# GitHub token the VM uses for gh and git push (SSH agent forwarding is off,
# so your laptop's key isn't available there). A fine-grained PAT:
#   https://github.com/settings/personal-access-tokens/new
#   Resource owner: the org or user owning the repos (e.g. dembrane)
#   Repository access: only the repos in SF_FACTORY_REPOS
#   Permissions: Contents read/write, Pull requests read/write, Issues read
#                (Metadata read is added automatically)
#   Expiration: set one. An org may need to allow or approve the token.
# PRs and commits are made as you. This token could also merge, so branch
# protection on the default branch is what really stops an agent merging.
# Sent to the VM over stdin, never on a command line; stored there in gh's
# config (~/.config/gh/hosts.yml), since the VM has no keyring. A repo added
# to SF_FACTORY_REPOS must be added to the token too.
# SF_GH_TOKEN=""

# Git commit name and email on the VM. Empty: taken from the token's GitHub
# account, with its private noreply address.
# SF_GH_NAME=""
# SF_GH_EMAIL=""
